    "body": "# Vulnerability details\n\n## Referenced Code Locations\n\n**LatentSwapLogic.sol:**\n- ETWAP update logic in `_calculateMarketState()`:\nhttps://github.com/code-423n4/2025-10-covenant/blob/d5ebe4461564b46cacf8a90cf11add29470ef001/src/lex/latentswap/libraries/LatentSwapLogic.sol#L879-L891\n- Redemption cap check in `_checkRedeemCap()`:\nhttps://github.com/code-423n4/2025-10-covenant/blob/d5ebe4461564b46cacf8a90cf11add29470ef001/src/lex/latentswap/libraries/LatentSwapLogic.sol#L1081-L1099\n- Redemption logic in `_calcRedeem()`:\nhttps://github.com/code-423n4/2025-10-covenant/blob/d5ebe4461564b46cacf8a90cf11add29470ef001/src/lex/latentswap/libraries/LatentSwapLogic.sol#L364-L497\n\n## Finding description and impact\n\n### Root Cause\n\nThe protocol uses an Exponential Time-Weighted Average Price (ETWAP) mechanism to limit how fast users can redeem tokens. The redemption cap is calculated as:\n\n```solidity\n// Maximum redemption = current market size - (ETWAP × 0.75)\n// This means after redemption, market must still be ≥ 75% of ETWAP\n```\n\nThe vulnerability exists because ETWAP updates **on every transaction** based on elapsed time since the last transaction:\n\n```solidity\n// In _calculateMarketState()\nif (block.timestamp > marketState.lexState.lastUpdateTimestamp) {\n    vars.elapsedTime = block.timestamp - marketState.lexState.lastUpdateTimestamp;\n    \n    // ETWAP updates using exponential decay with 30-minute half-life\n    vars.invUpdateFactor = DebtMath.calculateApproxExponentialUpdate(\n        LN2, vars.elapsedTime, ETWAP_MIN_HALF_LIFE\n    );\n    \n    // ETWAP moves toward current market size\n    marketState.lexState.lastETWAPBaseSupply = /* updated based on elapsed time */;\n}\n```\n\nUsers can force ETWAP updates by initiating transactions. Each update moves ETWAP closer to the current (smaller) market size, which unlocks additional redemption capacity.\n\n### The Intended Behavior\n\nBased on the code documentation:\n\n```solidity\nuint8 constant MAX_REDEEM_FACTOR_CAP = 2; \n// 1/4 max liquidity burn as a % of total market liquidity (measured through ETWAP). \n// ie, limits amount that can be burned in ETWAP_MIN_HALF_LIFE minutes\n\nuint16 constant ETWAP_MIN_HALF_LIFE = 30 minutes;\n```\n\nThe design intends to limit redemptions to approximately 25% of ETWAP every 30 minutes, resulting in roughly **50% maximum redemption rate per hour**.\n\nThe comment in `_checkRedeemCap()` confirms this:\n```solidity\n// But for bigger markets, approx. ~MAX_REDEEM_FACTOR_CAP/2 can be redeemed every 1hr\n// (this is based on ETWAP_MIN_HALF_LIFE and MAX_REDEEM_FACTOR_CAP values)\n```\n\n### What Actually Happens\n\n**Single Large Redemption (Intended Path):**\n```\nt=0:     Market=1000, ETWAP=1000 → Redeem 250 (25%)\nt=0:     Market=750, ETWAP=1000 (unchanged) → Cannot redeem more (blocked)\nt=30min: Market=750, ETWAP=875 (updated) → Can redeem ~93 more\nResult:  ~343 tokens in 30 minutes ≈ 50% per hour\n```\n\n**Multiple Small Redemptions (Exploit Path):**\n```\nt=0:    Market=1000, ETWAP=1000 → Redeem 100\nt=5min: Market=900, ETWAP=973 (updated!) → Redeem 20\nt=10min: Market=880, ETWAP=947 (updated!) → Redeem 20\n... continues every 5 minutes for 12 transactions\nResult: 717 tokens in 60 minutes = 71% per hour\n```\n\nThe frequent small transactions force ETWAP to update continuously, each time unlocking more redemption capacity.\n\n### Impact\n\n- Creates systematic unfair advantage for users exploiting the issue\n- Regular users making single redemptions: ~30-40% exit rate per hour\n- Users exploiting the issue (redemptions every 5 minutes): **71% exit rate per hour**\n- Speed advantage: **2.8x faster exit** than regular users\n- Accelerates market collapse instead of preventing it\n\n**Real-World Example:**\nIn a $10M market during crisis:\n- Regular user: Exits with ~$3.5M in first hour (35%)\n- User exploiting the issue: Exits with **$7.1M in first hour (71%)**\n- **Advantage: $3.6M more extracted by exploiting the bypass**\n\n### Mathematical Proof of Bypass\n\nThe contradiction that proves this is a bypass:\n\n```solidity\n// Scenario A: Try to redeem 717 tokens in one transaction\ncovenant.redeem(717 tokens) \n→ REVERTS with E_LEX_RedeemCapExceeded\n\n// Scenario B: Redeem 717 tokens across 12 small transactions (5 min apart)\nfor (12 iterations) {\n    covenant.redeem(~60 tokens)\n    wait 5 minutes\n}\n→ SUCCESS! Total redeemed: 717 tokens\n```\n\nIf the rate limit is working correctly, why can 717 tokens be redeemed across multiple transactions but not in one? This proves the mechanism is being bypassed.\n\n### Why This is Not Intended Behavior\n\n1. **Code documentation explicitly states the limit:** \"limits amount that can be burned in ETWAP_MIN_HALF_LIFE minutes\"\n2. **Calculation shows ~50% per hour maximum:** Based on 25% every 30 minutes with half-life decay\n3. **Actual results exceed this by 42%:** 71% achieved vs 50% intended\n4. **The mechanism's purpose is defeated:** Designed to prevent fast exits during bank runs, but allows 2.8x faster exits through timing games\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\n### Solution 1: Per-Address Redemption Cooldown\n\nAdd a cooldown period between redemptions for each address:\n\n```solidity\n// In LatentSwapLEX.sol or Covenant.sol\nmapping(address => uint256) public lastRedemptionTime;\nuint256 constant REDEMPTION_COOLDOWN = 15 minutes;\n\nmodifier redeemCooldown() {\n    require(\n        block.timestamp >= lastRedemptionTime[msg.sender] + REDEMPTION_COOLDOWN,\n        \"Redemption on cooldown\"\n    );\n    lastRedemptionTime[msg.sender] = block.timestamp;\n    _;\n}\n\nfunction redeem(RedeemParams calldata redeemParams) \n    external \n    payable \n    override \n    noDelegateCall \n    lock(redeemParams.marketId)\n    redeemCooldown  // Add this modifier\n    returns (uint256) \n{\n    // ... existing logic\n}\n```\n\n**Benefits:**\n- Prevents gaming ETWAP update frequency\n- Allows legitimate redemptions\n- Simple implementation\n- Minimal gas overhead\n- 15-minute cooldown aligns with the 30-minute ETWAP half-life\n\n**Limitation:** Users could split across multiple addresses, but this increases friction and gas costs significantly.",
